  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An anomalously strong upper level ridge is \nforecast to spread east from the OH/TN Valleys into the \nCarolinas/Mid- Atlantic Region this week. Meanwhile, anomalous \nlow-level thicknesses beneath the ridge, plus strong heating and\npersistent southerly flow, point towards the potential for \nunusually hot conditions for the area. In fact, some of our more\nreliable forecast aids point towards this being an outlier type\nof event from a climatology standpoint, with record highs \npossible. \n\nThe heat is forecast to peak today through Friday. However, \nthere are some caveats worth pointing out. There is the \npotential for a weak front or convective outflow to dip south \ninto our northern counties today. Should this occur, a not-as-\nhot easterly onshore flow may hold highs down some around the \nAlbemarle Sound and Northern OBX vicinity. One other potential \ncaveat is thunderstorms. With subsidence aloft, the seabreeze \nshould be fairly muted this week. However, any convection that \ncan develop upstream would have a tendency to propagate towards \nENC thanks to the west/northwest flow aloft. Any convection that\nsurvives into the area could hold temps down some, especially \nwith any afternoon convection. For now, these are not expected \nto be significant limiting factors, and even if so, it would \nmost likely only impact a relatively smaller area of ENC. \n\nIn light of this, confidence is high regarding anomalous heat \nand humidity, with 2-3 days of significant heat impacts \nexpected. In fact, the NWS' experimental heat risk guidance \nshows most of ENC in the \"Major\" to \"Extreme\" category for heat.\n\nRegarding messaging, a Heat Advisory continues for most of ENC \ntoday. For now, we have left out the central and northern OBX \nin light of the potential for a brief period of easterly flow to\ndevelop which would keep temps down some. Additional heat \nheadlines are likely through the week. As was mentioned \nyesterday, it's less common for heat headlines to be issued in \nthe month of September in our forecast area. Today's Heat \nAdvisory is the first heat headline issued in September since \n2020. Additionally, please see the \"CLIMATE\" section below for \nrecord information at select sites through Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong cold front is forecast to encroach on \nthe Carolinas late Friday, then push fully through ENC on \nSaturday. Ahead of the front, guidance is mixed on whether or \nnot convection will manage to develop ahead of the front over, \nor upstream of, ENC Friday afternoon and evening. Upper level \nridging may hold on one more day, but the signal in some \nguidance can't be ignored. In light of this, the forecast will \nshow modest thunderstorm probabilities Friday afternoon and \nevening (30-50% chance). The synoptic setup appears fairly \nfavorable for strong to severe thunderstorms on Friday if \nridging doesn't limit deep convection. Most machine learning and\nprobabilistic guidance favor this potential as well, and SPC \nhas outlooked a portion of the area in a Slight Risk. The risk \nof convection continues on Saturday, but the evolution may hinge\non Friday's convection.\n\nBehind Saturday's front, highs are forecast to get bumped down \nto the 80s, with lows in the 60s. This will be a notable drop \ncompared to the heat of the next few days. It may not end up \nquite as dry as some guidance previously forecast, but dewpoints\nin the 60s won't feel as oppressive as the next few days.",
